{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794046772,"nonce":"+/hpVnPVu0SQJ0/jdqtCmskbbTc=","title":"GC: Immix-style heap for the GC-managed mode, from wren_lift and ash","message":"Zyntax's collector is a conservative, non-moving mark-sweep over size-class slabs. Neither sibling runtime moves objects, and both converged on the same Immix layout. This records what they do and what transfers. The survey in docs/WREN_LIFT_GC_SURVEY.md says wren_lift has no Immix; that is out of date (gc_immix.rs and gc_immix_heap.rs, default since 2026-09, the only collector since commit 23db62f).\n\nShared design (ash std/src/gc.rs, wren_lift src/runtime/gc_immix_heap.rs):\n- 32 KiB blocks, 128 B lines, 16 B quantum. Objects up to a line bump-allocate and never straddle a line. Larger objects own whole lines and never share the last one.\n- Side tables, no per-block header: one start byte per quantum (size in quanta, or a span marker), a line count per span start, a 256-bit line-mark bitmap per block. Any address inside an allocation resolves to its start by walking back in the line, then across spans, gated by a per-block has_span flag.\n- Sweep outcomes per block: no marked line frees the block; otherwise maximal runs of unmarked lines become bump regions. Reuse zeroes the run and clears its start bytes.\n- Conservative stacks with a callee-saved register spill; fiber stacks from their saved sp.\n- Non-moving on purpose. wren_lift deleted its generational, copying collector because it needed stack maps, write barriers, root writebacks and a frame-pointer fixup; conservative scanning removed that bug class.\n\nLessons that cost them something:\n- Line recycling is required, not optional. Block-only reclamation fragmented without bound because one pointer-shaped integer pins a block: 1132 MB held for 195 MB live, pauses up to 362 ms. Recycling brought it to about 42% occupancy of retained blocks (ash 5f0aaaed).\n- Trace per object, never per line. Line-granular tracing scanned unrelated neighbours: retention chains over 227,000 edges; rounding allocations to whole lines as a control dropped peak retention from 512 MB to 1.6 MB (ash 842d3320). Two reachable objects on a shared line are both traced; an unreachable neighbour is not.\n- One object per line wasted 5x footprint (ash 0ced4eba). Pack at 16 B.\n- Enabled recycling only after the suite passed with a collection on every allocation (ash ASH_GC_STRESS; Zyntax has ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1).\n- Pointer-free blocks are separate and never scanned (ash noptr). Marking is memory-latency bound, 88 to 92% of the pause.\n- Hand memory back only after a quiet collection (no collection for 30 s, or idle 300 ms); per-sweep madvise cost ash 17% on mandelbrot. On macOS keep MADV_FREE_REUSABLE and the reuse call paired or the footprint inflates.\n- Compiled code bumps the thread's region inline, writing the start byte (ash 178f3a95, wren_lift 21a4c53).\n- Mark colours alternate per cycle, so survivors need no reset pass (wren_lift).\n\nPacing, the part that touches 3bbb229: wren_lift triggers at live x 4 clamped to [8 MB, max(512 MB, live)]. Ash uses live x growth, growth adaptive in [4, 16] against a 5% GC time share, clamped to [8 MB, min(ceiling, max(2 x live, 128 MB))], with ceiling from RAM, and measures live as used blocks, not marked bytes. Its notes: a ceiling that scales with the heap let a 1 GB live set pile up 2 GB of garbage (peak over 5 GB); a ceiling below live collects continuously; deferring collection let mandelbrot reach 590 MB RSS with 3.5 MB live. Ash also runs sticky-mark minors with a card-marking barrier, which makes its cap affordable; the cap cost Zyntax's full-mark collector 40 to 55% on a table build (3bbb229), so it is not free here.\n\nWhat does not transfer as is:\n- Zyntax's default is drop-site release into per-class free lists, with the collector as a backstop. Immix has no per-object free. It belongs to the GC-managed mode (set_gc_managed_drops), where a release would be a no-op. Decide that before building.\n- Blocks are headerless, so the mark bit lives in the start byte (as in ash), not in an object header (as in wren_lift).\n- A pointer one past an object names the next one in ash too (strict bound), which matches Zyntax's rule that compiled code keeps a buffer's base live.\n\nOpen for the design: pointer-free allocation hint from the allocation intrinsic (strings, flat arrays); inline bump in compiled code; generational minors and a barrier are a later step, wren_lift chose none.","files":null}]}